<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <artifactId>jmsfx-viewer</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>
    <name>JMSFX Viewer Applications</name>
    <description>The applications that compose and display symbols - a desktop icon creator and a web server - each consuming a generated icon library like any other consumer.</description>

    <parent>
        <groupId>io.github.ctgnz</groupId>
        <artifactId>jmsfx-parent</artifactId>
        <version>2.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../jmsfx-parent/pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <!--
        These two are the same capability with two front ends, so they share a release: whatever the
        creator can compose, the site should be able to compose too, and a version that means one but
        not the other would be misleading. Grouping them keeps ${project.version} doing that work.

        This is also the level at which a deliverable gets built, so it is where jmsfx#112 puts the
        per-library profiles: neither application compiles against a library any more (jmsfx#76,
        jmsfx#93), so which one it ships with is a packaging decision made here rather than a
        compile-time dependency inherited by each child.
    -->
    <modules>
        <module>jmsfx-creator</module>
        <module>jmsfx-server</module>
    </modules>

    <!--
        One profile per library, and the library is always named explicitly: -Pstandard, -Phallux.
        Both applications inherit whichever is active, because a dependency declared in a profile
        here reaches every child - which is the point of putting them at this level rather than
        repeating them in two poms.

        Deliberately no activeByDefault. A default-activated profile switches off the moment any
        other profile is named, and jmsfx#103 put `mvn -Prelease deploy` in the release workflow, so
        a defaulted `standard` would silently deactivate during a release and publish an application
        with no library in it. That failure is quiet and late: the jar starts, then answers 500 to
        every request with "No IconLibrary on the classpath". The enforcer rule below is what turns
        naming none into an immediate, explanatory failure instead.

        runtime scope, and *not* optional. spring-boot:repackage leaves optional dependencies out of
        BOOT-INF/lib, so an optional library never reaches the server's fat jar (jmsfx#93). The
        creator got away with optional because dependency:copy-dependencies does include optional
        dependencies - but optional only stops a dependency reaching consumers, and since jmsfx#109
        nothing consumes either application as a library. So it earns nothing in either, and having
        it in one and not the other was a trap.

        jmsfx-battleorder has no profile yet: its model is an overlay and there is no generated
        library to depend on until jmsfx#81 can compose one. Adding it is then a copy of the hallux
        profile plus a jmsfx.battleorder.version property.
    -->
    <profiles>
        <profile>
            <id>standard</id>
            <properties>
                <jmsfx.library>standard</jmsfx.library>
            </properties>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>io.github.ctgnz</groupId>
                    <artifactId>jmsfx-standard</artifactId>
                    <version>${jmsfx.standard.version}</version>
                    <scope>runtime</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
        </profile>
        <profile>
            <id>hallux</id>
            <properties>
                <jmsfx.library>hallux</jmsfx.library>
            </properties>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>io.github.ctgnz</groupId>
                    <artifactId>jmsfx-hallux</artifactId>
                    <version>${jmsfx.hallux.version}</version>
                    <scope>runtime</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <!--
                    Naming no library has to fail, rather than produce an application that builds
                    green and then cannot serve a single request. Every profile above sets
                    jmsfx.library, so its absence means none of them is active.

                    This does mean `mvn verify` at the repository root now needs -Pstandard. That is
                    the cost of having no silent default, and it is deliberate: the alternative is a
                    build that guesses, and the wrong guess is only discovered in production.
                -->
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>require-a-library</id>
                        <phase>validate</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>enforce</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <rules>
                                <requireProperty>
                                    <property>jmsfx.library</property>
                                    <message>No symbology library was named, so this build has nothing to render with.

Name one on the command line:

    mvn -Pstandard verify        the APP-6E library
    mvn -Phallux   verify        the hallux extension

There is deliberately no default - see the comment above this rule, and jmsfx#112.</message>
                                </requireProperty>
                            </rules>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
